{"cuisine/centrafrican-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/centrafrican-cuisine","n":"Centrafrican cuisine","c":"#e46a3f","crumb":[["Africa","cuisines#africa"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Central African Rep.","intro":[["Central African cuisine includes the cuisines, cooking traditions, practices, ingredients and foods of the Central African Republic (CAR). Indigenous agriculture in the country includes millet, sorghum, banana, yam, okra, yellow onion, garlic, spinach, rice and palm oil. Imported crops of American origin include maize, manioc (cassava), peanuts, chili peppers, sweet potato and tomato. Additional foods include onions, garlic, chiles and peanuts."],["Meat can be scarce in the Central African Republic, although fish is used in a variety of dishes, and other sources of protein include peanuts and insects such as cicadas, grasshoppers, crickets and termites. Common meats in Central African cuisine include chicken and goat. Wild game is also hunted, especially in rural areas and during the grass-burning dry season. Staple foods include starches, such as millet, rice, sesame and sorghum. A variety of vegetables and sauces are also consumed."],["Roadside stalls sell foods such as baked goods and makara (a type of fried bread), sandwiches, barbecued meat and snacks. In the forests and in markets of Bangui where forest items are sold, caterpillars and the koko leaf are eaten. Restaurants are mostly for expatriates. Wild tubers, leaves, and mushrooms are used. Palm oil is widely used in various dishes."],["The capital city of Bangui has western foods and hotel restaurants. The legal drinking age is 18. Muslims are prohibited from drinking alcohol. The PK5 area is known for its smaller restaurants serving reasonably priced traditional dishes."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"centrafrican-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Centrafrican_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]}],"cont":"African cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/7/70/Boulettes_ti_k%C3%B6ss%C3%B4_en_Rca.jpg/500px-Boulettes_ti_k%C3%B6ss%C3%B4_en_Rca.jpg","w":500,"h":500,"by":"Bile rene","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boulettes_ti_k%C3%B6ss%C3%B4_en_Rca.jpg"}},"cuisine/indian-fast-food":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/indian-fast-food","n":"Indian fast food","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["The fast food industry in India has evolved with the changing lifestyles of the young Indian population. The variety of gastronomic preferences across the regions, hereditary or acquired, has brought about different modules across the country."],["Many of the traditional dishes have been adapted to suit the emerging fast food outlets. The basic adaptation is to decrease the processing and serving time. For example, the typical meal which called for being served by an ever-alert attendant is now offered as a Mini-Meal across the counter. In its traditional version, a plate or a banana leaf was first laid down on the floor or table. Several helpers then waited on the diner, doling out different dishes and refilling as they got over in the plate."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"indian-fast-food"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_fast_food"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Chaat: Recipes from the Kitchens, Markets, and Railways of India","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Maneet Chauhan and Jody Eddy, 2020","note":"Indian street snacks and chaat gathered on a train journey across India.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781984823885","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781984823885"},{"name":"India Local: Classic Street Food Recipes","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Sonal Ved","note":"Regional Indian street food recipes by a Mumbai-based food writer.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9789392130427","suggested":true,"isbn":"9789392130427"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/qatari-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/qatari-cuisine","n":"Qatari cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Eastern Arabian cuisine","cuisine/eastern-arabian-cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Qatar","intro":[["Qatari cuisine is made up of traditional Arab cuisine. Machbūs, a meal consisting of rice, meat, and vegetables, is the national dish in Qatar, typically made with either lamb or chicken and slow-cooked to give it a depth of flavour. Seafood and dates are staple food items in the country. Many of these dishes are also used in other countries in the region, as they share many commonalities. In other parts of the region, some of the dishes have different names or use slightly different ingredients."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"qatari-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qatari_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Odehlicious (Khaleeji)","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"theodehlicious.com","note":"Khaleeji section of a Middle Eastern home cooking blog.","url":"https://theodehlicious.com/cuisines/khaleeji/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Cardamom and Lime: Recipes from the Arabian Gulf","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Sarah al-Hamad","note":"Home cooking from the Gulf states as a whole, not this country alone.","url":"https://www.simonandschuster.ca/books/Cardamom-and-Lime/Sarah-al-Hamad/9781566567251","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781566567251"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Eastern Arabian cuisine","cuisine/eastern-arabian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/4/46/Doha_-_food.jpg/500px-Doha_-_food.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Justine.toms","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Doha_-_food.jpg"}},"cuisine/extremaduran-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/extremaduran-cuisine","n":"Extremaduran cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Spain","intro":[["Extremadura, Spain is known for its different ways of preparing the Iberian pork and mutton. The main characteristics of the traditional Extremaduran cuisine are its simplicity, its lack of clutter and its low cost. It is also a cuisine reflecting a generous spirit, for many of its preparations used to be cooked in large pots to share with visitors, friends, and neighbors. The resulting dishes are eaten with local bread."],["The preferred spices in Extremaduran cuisine are paprika (pimentón), garlic, bay leaves, pennyroyal, and anise. The Northeastern comarca of La Vera produces pimentón de la Vera, smoked paprika highly valued all over Spain and extensively used in Extremaduran cuisine. Olive oil is used for frying and as an ingredient in many dishes. Maybe due to Portuguese influence, some limited use of coriander leaves (unusual for other regions of mainland Spain outside the Canary Islands) can be found for some specific dishes such as repápalos or pickled cod (escabeche de bacalao), though often with the possibility of using parsley as an alternative and not with the same extensive use as in Portugal."],["Wine is produced mainly in the territory of the Ribera del Guadiana denominación de origen. The region is also known for its vino de pitarra tradition, home-made wine made in small earthenware vessels."],["The Jerte Valley is known for its cherry production, in the local picota del Jerte varieties, highly regarded all over Spain and with a denominación de origen."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"extremaduran-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extremaduran_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/8/8d/Sopadeantruejo.jpg/500px-Sopadeantruejo.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Aceuchal","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sopadeantruejo.jpg"}},"cuisine/amazonian-kichwa-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/amazonian-kichwa-foodways","n":"Amazonian Kichwa foodways","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Napo and Pastaza, Ecuadorian Amazon","names":"Kichwa","intro":[["Kichwa families in Ecuador's Amazon grow food in the chakra, a forest garden that FAO lists as a Globally Important Agricultural Heritage System. In Alto Tena, a chakra holds lumu, ",["yuca","ingredient/cassava"],", kakau, ",["cacao","ingredient/theobroma-cacao"],", and its wild cousins, chunta palm fruit, kumal, ",["sweet potato","ingredient/sweet-potato"],", uchu muyu chiles, manduru, ",["achiote","ingredient/annatto"],", and dozens of forest fruits and nuts."],["Visitors are welcomed with ",["Chicha","dish/chicha"]," of yuca, which women mostly make. The boiled roots are mashed in a wooden trough, chewed and returned so saliva breaks down the starch, then left to ferment for a couple of days or longer, thinned with water and served in a clay bowl called a mucawa. ",["Guayusa","ingredient/guayusa"],", a native leaf, is traditionally brewed and sipped in the morning, and Kichwa entrepreneur Esthela Noteno now bottles it from her grandmother's recipe."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"FAO, Amazonian chakra, a traditional agroforestry system in Napo, Ecuador","url":"https://www.fao.org/giahs/around-the-world/detail/ecuador-amazonia"},{"name":"Field Museum, Edible plants of the Kichwa chakra, Alto Tena","url":"https://fieldguides.fieldmuseum.org/sites/default/files/rapid-color-guides-pdfs/1113_ecuador_edible_plants_of_the_kichwa_chakra_kichwa_at_the_alto_tena_community_0.pdf"},{"name":"Sounds and Colours, Chicha, an ancestral beverage to feed body and soul","url":"https://soundsandcolours.com/articles/ecuador/chicha-an-ancestral-beverage-to-feed-body-and-soul-7430/"},{"name":"World Wildlife Fund, In Ecuador, a natural beverage is rooted in Indigenous tradition","url":"https://www.worldwildlife.org/news/magazine/winter-2024/in-ecuador-a-natural-beverage-is-rooted-in-indigenous-tradition/"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"amazonian-kichwa-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Chicha","dish/chicha"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Cassava","ingredient/cassava"],["Maize","ingredient/maize"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/1/17/Chicha_with_Pipe%C3%B1o.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"simenon simenon","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chicha_with_Pipe%C3%B1o.jpg","of":"Chicha"}},"ingredient/garlic":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/garlic","n":"Garlic","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 64 dishes across 50 cuisines","intro":[["Garlic (Allium sativum) is a species of bulbous flowering plants in the genus Allium. Its close relatives include the onion, shallot, leek, chives, Welsh onion, and Chinese onion. Garlic is native to Central Asia, northeastern Iran and the foothills of  Himalayas. It has naturalized in many other parts of the world, including Mediterranean Europe and China. There are two subspecies and hundreds of varieties of garlic."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-garlic"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garlic"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":50,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Arsik","dish/arsik"],["Bubur pedas","dish/bubur-pedas"],["Burgo (food)","dish/burgo-food"],["Empal gentong","dish/empal-gentong"],["Keumamah","dish/keumamah"],["Konro","dish/konro"],["Lawar (food)","dish/lawar-food"],["Lotek (food)","dish/lotek-food"],["Manuk napinadar","dish/manuk-napinadar"],["Mie ayam","dish/mie-ayam"],["Nasi goreng","dish/nasi-goreng"],["Semur (Indonesian stew)","dish/semur-indonesian-stew"],["Siomay","dish/siomay"]]],["Georgian cuisine","cuisine/georgian-cuisine",[["Ajika","dish/ajika"],["Chakapuli","dish/chakapuli"],["Chashushuli","dish/chashushuli"],["Kharcho","dish/kharcho"],["Kuchmachi","dish/kuchmachi"],["Nigvziani badrijani","dish/nigvziani-badrijani"],["Pkhali","dish/pkhali"],["Shkmeruli","dish/shkmeruli"]]],["Bulgarian cuisine","cuisine/bulgarian-cuisine",[["İmam bayıldı","dish/imam-bay-ld"],["Kyopolou","dish/kyopolou"],["Lyutenitsa","dish/lyutenitsa"],["Pindjur","dish/pindjur"],["Tarator","dish/tarator"]]],["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Agliata","dish/agliata"],["Cioppino","dish/cioppino"],["Marinara sauce","dish/marinara-sauce"],["Spaghetti alla Nerano","dish/spaghetti-alla-nerano"]]],["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine",[["Andrajos","dish/andrajos"],["Gazpacho","dish/gazpacho"],["Patatas a lo pobre","dish/patatas-a-lo-pobre"],["Salmorejo","dish/salmorejo"]]],["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Cioppino","dish/cioppino"],["Jibarito","dish/jibarito"],["Ranch dressing","dish/ranch-dressing"]]],["Macedonian cuisine","cuisine/macedonia